questions.stem:
A single ampersand is the non-short-circuit form of the logical and, so both operands are evaluated even when the left alone would settle the result. What does this program print?
static boolean mark(String tag, boolean value) {
    System.out.print(tag);
    return value;
}
boolean left = false;
boolean both = left & mark("X", true);
System.out.println("|both=" + both);

The single vertical bar is the non-short-circuit form of the logical or, so the right operand is evaluated even though the left is already true. What does this program print?
static boolean mark(String tag, boolean value) {
    System.out.print(tag);
    return value;
}
boolean left = true;
boolean either = left | mark("Y", true);
System.out.println("|either=" + either);

The logical not operator is unary, so a doubled not inverts the same flag twice in a row. What does this program print?
boolean ready = false;
boolean proceed = !!ready;
System.out.println("proceed=" + proceed);

The caret on two booleans computes the exclusive or, which is true only when the two operands differ. What does this program print?
static boolean mark(String tag, boolean value) {
    System.out.print(tag);
    return value;
}
boolean left = true;
boolean right = false;
boolean differs = left ^ mark("X", right);
System.out.println("|differs=" + differs);

Short-circuit evaluation is what makes a null test safe to pair with a dereference in one condition. What does this program print?
static int lengthOf(String text) {
    return text.length();
}
String maybe = null;
boolean safe = maybe != null && lengthOf(maybe) > 0;
System.out.println("safe=" + safe);

Short-circuit evaluation in a disjunction stops only when the left operand already settles the result. What does this program print?
static int lengthOf(String text) {
    return text.length();
}
String maybe = null;
boolean missing = maybe == null;
boolean safe = missing || lengthOf(maybe) > 0;
System.out.println("safe=" + safe);

Short-circuit evaluation only stops when the left operand already decides the result. What does this program print?
static boolean mark(String tag, boolean value) {
    System.out.print(tag);
    return value;
}
boolean left = true;
boolean both = left && mark("X", false);
System.out.println("|both=" + both);

Operator precedence decides whether the negation attaches to a single operand or to the whole conjunction. What does this program print?
boolean a = true;
boolean b = false;
boolean result = !a && b;
System.out.println("result=" + result);

In a chain mixing the two binary logical operators, precedence fixes the grouping without any parentheses. What does this program print?
boolean a = true;
boolean b = false;
boolean c = false;
boolean result = a || b && c;
System.out.println("result=" + result);

Parentheses override precedence, so the same three operands group differently here than in an unparenthesised chain. What does this program print?
boolean a = true;
boolean b = false;
boolean c = false;
boolean result = (a || b) && c;
System.out.println("result=" + result);

Additive operators sit higher in the precedence table than relational ones, so the sum is formed before the comparison. What does this program print?
int a = 1;
int b = 2;
int c = 4;
boolean small = a + b < c;
System.out.println("small=" + small);

Assignment has the lowest precedence of these operators, so an expression on its right is evaluated in full before the store happens. What does this program print?
int a = 1;
int b = 0;
boolean flag = a == b || a > 0;
System.out.println("flag=" + flag);

A student tries to write a range test as one chained expression and the compiler rejects the file. What is the actual reason?
int score = 7;
if (0 < score < 10) {
    System.out.println("in range");
}

An equality operator is happy to compare two booleans, so a comparison can be nested inside another. What does this program print?
int a = 3;
int b = 3;
int c = 4;
boolean same = (a == b) == (b == c);
System.out.println("same=" + same);

A char is one unsigned sixteen-bit code unit, so it can be compared directly against char literals in a range test. What does this program print?
char letter = 'M';
boolean upper = letter >= 'A' && letter <= 'Z';
System.out.println("upper=" + upper);

A library predicate and a hand-written range test answer the same question for a plain digit. What does this program print?
char c = '7';
boolean numeric = Character.isDigit(c);
boolean inRange = c >= '0' && c <= '9';
System.out.println(numeric + " " + inRange);

A character classifier is often used to filter the acceptable characters of an identifier. What does this program print?
char c = ' ';
boolean allowed = Character.isLetterOrDigit(c);
System.out.println("allowed=" + allowed);

An emptiness test on a string asks about length, not about visibility. What does this program print?
String text = "";
boolean blank = text.isEmpty();
System.out.println("blank=" + blank);

Two emptiness tests are compared on a value made only of spaces. What does this program print?
String text = "   ";
boolean empty = text.isEmpty();
boolean blank = text.isBlank();
System.out.println(empty + " " + blank);

The receiver of a method call is the object the method runs against, and a null receiver has no members to reach. What happens when this program runs?
String stored = null;
boolean same = stored.equals("hi");
System.out.println("same=" + same);

A static helper exists precisely because the instance method cannot be called on a null reference. What does this program print?
String stored = null;
boolean same = java.util.Objects.equals(stored, "hi");
System.out.println("same=" + same);

A case-insensitive comparison folds case before comparing characters. What does this program print?
String a = "Quiz";
String b = "QUIZ";
System.out.println(a.equalsIgnoreCase(b));

A comparison that returns an int encodes an ordering in the sign of the result, not a distance. What does this program print?
String a = "apple";
String b = "banana";
int order = a.compareTo(b);
System.out.println("order=" + (order < 0));

Folding case changes the ordering, and the plain comparison is shown beside it for contrast. What does this program print?
String a = "apple";
String b = "Banana";
System.out.println(a.compareTo(b) + " " + a.compareToIgnoreCase(b));

One comparison asks about the object, the other asks about the characters it holds. What does this program print?
StringBuilder buffer = new StringBuilder("hi");
String plain = "hi";
System.out.println(buffer.equals(plain) + " " + buffer.toString().contentEquals(plain));

A wrapper reference must be unwrapped before it can act as a condition, and a null wrapper has nothing to unwrap. What happens when this program runs?
Boolean ready = null;
if (ready) {
    System.out.println("go");
}

A switch must read its selector before it can compare it against any label. What happens when this program runs?
String key = null;
String name = switch (key) {
    case "a" -> "first";
    default -> "other";
};
System.out.println(name);

An enum constant is an ordinary reference, and a reference may be null. What happens when this program runs?
enum Level { LOW, HIGH }
Level level = null;
String label = switch (level) {
    case LOW -> "low";
    case HIGH -> "high";
};
System.out.println(label);

The position of a default rule in the block does not decide when it is consulted. What does this program print?
int code = 9;
String label = switch (code) {
    default -> "other";
    case 1 -> "one";
    case 2 -> "two";
};
System.out.println(label);

A value switch evaluates its selector once and picks the one rule whose label matches, so position in the block is beside the point. What does this program print?
int code = 1;
String label = switch (code) {
    case 9 -> "nine";
    case 1 -> "one";
    case 5 -> "five";
    default -> "other";
};
System.out.println(label);

A switch block is rejected by the compiler because of the way its labels are written. What is the actual reason?
int code = 2;
String label = switch (code) {
    case 2 -> "two";
    case 2 -> "again";
    default -> "other";
};
System.out.println(label);

The compiler rejects a switch because one of its labels is not a constant expression. What is the actual reason?
int one = 1;
int code = 1;
String label = switch (code) {
    case one -> "one";
    default -> "other";
};
System.out.println(label);

A label is written with the wrong kind of constant and the compiler rejects the file. What is the actual reason?
int code = 1;
String label = switch (code) {
    case "a" -> "first";
    default -> "other";
};
System.out.println(label);

A value is asked for from a conditional expression whose two branches produce nothing, and the compiler refuses. What is the actual reason?
static void first() {
    System.out.print("a");
}

static void second() {
    System.out.print("b");
}
boolean pick = true;
int size = pick ? first() : second();
System.out.println(size);

In the colon form a group of statements runs to completion and then continues into the next label unless something stops it. What does this program print?
int code = 1;
StringBuilder log = new StringBuilder();
switch (code) {
    case 1:
        log.append("one");
    case 2:
        log.append("two");
        break;
    default:
        log.append("other");
}
System.out.println(log);

A char is an allowed selector type, and each label is a char constant. What does this program print?
char grade = 'B';
String label = switch (grade) {
    case 'A' -> "excellent";
    case 'B' -> "good";
    default -> "other";
};
System.out.println(label);

When every constant of an enum has a rule, a switch expression is complete without a default rule. What does this program print?
enum Level { LOW, HIGH }
Level level = Level.HIGH;
String label = switch (level) {
    case LOW -> "low";
    case HIGH -> "high";
};
System.out.println(label);

The statement form of a switch may legitimately do nothing when no label matches. What does this program print?
enum Level { LOW, HIGH }
Level level = Level.HIGH;
String label = "unset";
switch (level) {
    case LOW -> label = "low";
    case HIGH -> label = "high";
}
System.out.println(label);

A block-form rule needs a way to hand a value out of the block, because the block itself is not an expression. What does this program print?
int code = 1;
String label = switch (code) {
    case 1 -> {
        String temp = "one";
        yield temp;
    }
    default -> "other";
};
System.out.println(label);

The compiler rejects a switch expression because one of its rules cannot produce a value. What is the actual reason?
int code = 1;
String label = switch (code) {
    case 1 -> {
        String temp = "one";
    }
    default -> "other";
};
System.out.println(label);

A default rule may reject a value instead of naming it, and that is often the right way to exhaust a switch expression. What happens when this program runs?
int code = 7;
String label = switch (code) {
    case 1 -> "one";
    default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("bad code " + code);
};
System.out.println(label);

Matching a string label is an exact comparison of the characters, with no case folding applied. What does this program print?
String key = "A";
int size = switch (key) {
    case "a" -> 1;
    default -> 0;
};
System.out.println(size);

A byte is one of the permitted selector types, and its labels are written as int constants. What does this program print?
byte level = 2;
String label = switch (level) {
    case 1 -> "low";
    case 2 -> "mid";
    default -> "high";
};
System.out.println(label);

Reference comparison on strings is decided by which object each name points at, not by the characters. What does this program print?
String first = "quiz";
String second = "quiz";
System.out.println(first == second);

A set of small permissions is often packed into one int, with one bit per flag. What does this program print?
final int READ = 1;
final int WRITE = 2;
int flags = READ | 4;
System.out.println((flags & WRITE) != 0);

Adding a flag to a packed permission mask is done by or-ing in its bit. What does this program print?
final int READ = 1;
final int WRITE = 2;
int flags = READ;
flags |= WRITE;
System.out.println(flags);

Removing one permission from a packed mask has to leave the other permissions alone. What does this program print?
final int READ = 1;
final int WRITE = 2;
int flags = READ | WRITE;
flags &= ~WRITE;
System.out.println(flags);

The exclusive or flips a bit rather than setting or clearing it, which is what a toggle needs. What does this program print?
final int TRACK = 1;
int flags = TRACK;
flags ^= TRACK;
System.out.println(flags);

A 32-bit complement looks like an arithmetic trick until the bit width is taken into account. What does this program print?
int mask = 0;
System.out.println((~mask) + " " + (~mask == -1));

Two right shifts differ in what they drag into the vacated high positions. What does this program print?
int value = -8;
System.out.println((value >> 1) + " " + (value >>> 1));

A conditional expression evaluates its test and then only the branch it chooses. What does this program print?
static int calls() {
    System.out.print("T");
    return 10;
}
int divisor = 0;
int result = divisor == 0 ? calls() : 100 / divisor;
System.out.println("|result=" + result);

The parenthesised expression is evaluated on its own before the surrounding concatenation sees it. What does this program print?
int whole = 7;
int part = 2;
String text = "" + (whole / part) + "r";
System.out.println(text);

A conditional expression is right-associative, so a chain of them nests toward the right. What does this program print?
int score = 55;
String band = score >= 90 ? "A" : score >= 80 ? "B" : score >= 70 ? "C" : "F";
System.out.println(band);

A brace-less if followed by another brace-less if and an else has a binding rule that surprises people. What does this program print?
int marks = 5;
if (marks > 0)
    if (marks > 100)
        System.out.println("high");
    else
        System.out.println("not high");

A test whose value cannot change is not really a decision, and the compiler may act on that. What does this program print?
final int limit = 3;
int count = 5;
if (limit > 10) {
    count = 0;
}
System.out.println("count=" + count);

The right-hand operand of a conjunction can be a genuinely dangerous operation if it is reached. What does this program print?
int a = 12;
int b = 0;
boolean divisible = b != 0 && a % b == 0;
System.out.println("divisible=" + divisible);

A chain of three tests with increments inside them needs both the grouping and the short-circuit rule applied in order. What does this program print?
int calls = 0;
boolean chain = (++calls > 0) && (++calls > 5) && (++calls > 2);
System.out.println("calls=" + calls);

A disjunction skips its right operand only when the left already answers the question. What does this program print?
int calls = 0;
boolean any = (++calls > 0) || (++calls > 5);
System.out.println("calls=" + calls);

A type test in a condition is what makes a cast safe to write in the same expression. What does this program print?
Object value = "text";
String trimmed = value instanceof String ? ((String) value).trim() : "";
System.out.println("[" + trimmed + "]");

A conjunction of three operands is still left-associative, and each operand here changes a counter. What does this program print?
int calls = 0;
boolean chain = (++calls > 0) && (++calls > 1) && (++calls > 2);
System.out.println("calls=" + calls);
